---
import SealedSection from '../seal/SealedSection.astro';
import delimiterSeal from '../../assets/concepts/delimiter-seal.svg?raw';

const sealText =
  'Every block of context carries a random nonce and a SHA-256 integrity hash of its enclosed content. Anything unsealed is stripped at egress. The page you are reading is built out of the same device: each section header hashes its own sealed payload.';
---

<SealedSection label="sealed" sealText={sealText} id="sealed">
  <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
    <div class="prose-measure">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-vx-ink">The sealed block, explained.</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        Every block of context carries a random nonce and a SHA-256 integrity hash of its enclosed
        content. On the way out, any block with a missing nonce, an unknown nonce, or a failing hash
        is stripped before it reaches transport.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        The real claim: <strong>a model cannot widen its own advertised tool surface</strong> by writing a
        forged <code class="mono">&lt;tools&gt;</code> block. The markup is verified, not trusted.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-vx-ink/80">
        This page is built out of the same device. Each section is framed as a harness block whose
        header hash is computed at build time from that section&rsquo;s own sealed payload. Scroll one
        into view and its integrity badge runs a verify tick. Nothing here is trusted on sight.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-vx-ink p-2" aria-hidden="true">
      <Fragment set:html={delimiterSeal} />
    </div>
  </div>
</SealedSection>